<div class="topic-landing topic-landing--three-responses">
  <section class="topic-landing__hero" aria-labelledby="topic-three-heading">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="topic-landing__hero-copy">
        <p class="topic-landing__eyebrow">Christian Transhumanist Association</p>
        <h1 id="topic-three-heading">Three ways Christians respond to technology</h1>
        <p class="topic-landing__lede">
          New technology, and AI above all, demands a response. Most Christian responses fall into one of two patterns: keep up with it, or keep it out. There is a third option, older than both and more demanding: take hold of technology creatively, and turn it toward the work of God.
        </p>
        <div class="topic-landing__actions">
          <a class="btn btn-primary btn-lg" href="#create">See the third option</a>
          <a class="btn btn-default btn-lg" href="#questions">Ask a question</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <aside class="topic-landing__signal topic-landing__measure" aria-label="Three responses">
        <p class="topic-landing__signal-label">Three responses</p>
        <ol>
          <li><span>Keep Up</span> Technology is a trend. Stay current with it.</li>
          <li><span>Resist</span> Technology is a threat. Guard against it.</li>
          <li><span>Create</span> Technology is a calling. Take it up.</li>
        </ol>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="topic-landing__band" aria-labelledby="topic-three-frame">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="topic-landing__section-label">Why it matters</p>
      <h2 id="topic-three-frame">Every response carries an answer.</h2>
      <div class="topic-landing__prose topic-landing__measure">
        <p>How a church, a family, or a person responds to technology depends on what they think technology <em>is</em>. That question is rarely asked out loud, but the answer is always there underneath, shaping everything else.</p>
        <p>Each of the three responses below is held by thoughtful, faithful people, and each gets something right. But the first two rest on answers that are too small for what is actually happening. Seeing the answer underneath each response is the quickest way to see why a third option is needed.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="topic-landing__band" aria-labelledby="topic-three-keep-up">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="topic-landing__section-label">Response one</p>
      <h2 id="topic-three-keep-up">Keep Up: technology as a trend</h2>
      <div class="topic-landing__prose topic-landing__measure">
        <p>This is the church that livestreams, runs a sermon series on the latest app, and experiments with AI for announcements and outreach. Technology here is exciting and mostly neutral. The goal is relevance: meet people where they are, and don't get left behind.</p>
        <h3>What it gets right</h3>
        <p>Tools can serve the gospel, and the church has always used new media, from the codex to the printing press to radio. Refusing every new tool is its own kind of mistake.</p>
        <h3>Where it falls short</h3>
        <p>It treats technology as surface: a style to adopt, a wave to ride. It rarely asks what a technology is doing to the people who use it, or where it is headed. The market sets the agenda, and the church follows a step behind.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="topic-landing__band" aria-labelledby="topic-three-resist">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="topic-landing__section-label">Response two</p>
      <h2 id="topic-three-resist">Resist: technology as a threat</h2>
      <div class="topic-landing__prose topic-landing__measure">
        <p>This response sees technology as a rival to faith and to human nature: a new Tower of Babel, an idol that promises what only God can give, a force that dehumanizes. The goal is protection: limit screens, refuse enhancement, keep the machines at arm's length.</p>
        <h3>What it gets right</h3>
        <p>Technology really can dehumanize, concentrate power, and become an idol. The instinct to set limits, keep Sabbath, and ask hard questions before adopting something new is wisdom, not paranoia.</p>
        <h3>Where it falls short</h3>
        <p>It has no way to tell good technology from bad, only more from less. Christians already rely on medicine, eyeglasses, and writing itself; the Bible reached us through technology. And refusal does not stop a technology from being built. It only means the people building it will not be Christians.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="topic-landing__band topic-landing__band--ink" id="create" aria-labelledby="topic-three-create">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="topic-landing__section-label">Response three</p>
      <h2 id="topic-three-create">Create: technology as a calling</h2>
      <div class="topic-landing__prose topic-landing__measure">
        <p>The third response is <strong>Creative Engagement</strong>. Christians take hold of technology as agents, not as consumers following a trend or refugees fleeing one. We study it, build it, govern it, and direct it toward the good: toward healing, knowledge, connection, and life.</p>
        <p>This is not a compromise between the first two. It starts from a different answer to what technology is. From the first page of the Bible, humanity is called to cultivate creation, to observe it, name it, order it, and help it flourish. CTA calls this the <a href="/mission/theological/creation-mandate/">Creation Mandate</a>, and it has historically been understood as a calling to science and technology. On this view, technology is not a fad to chase or an enemy to fight. It is part of the work God gave us.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="topic-landing__principles">
        <article>
          <p class="topic-landing__number">01</p>
          <h3>Creative</h3>
          <p>We are made in the likeness of a Creator. Making new things is not a threat to our humanity; it is one of its clearest expressions.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p class="topic-landing__number">02</p>
          <h3>Agentic</h3>
          <p>We choose, shape, and govern what we build. That includes saying no. Creative Engagement keeps the discernment of the second response, but uses it to steer rather than to retreat.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p class="topic-landing__number">03</p>
          <h3>Appropriating</h3>
          <p>When Israel left Egypt, it carried Egyptian gold and silver, which later built the tabernacle. Augustine read that as a pattern: whatever is true and useful, wherever it comes from, can be claimed for God. Technology made for other ends can be taken up and turned to God's purposes.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <p class="topic-landing__number">04</p>
          <h3>Accountable</h3>
          <p>Taking up a calling means answering for how we carry it out. Creative Engagement takes the risks of technology seriously, because it takes responsibility for where technology goes.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="topic-landing__band" aria-labelledby="topic-three-next">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="topic-landing__section-label">The question underneath</p>
      <h2 id="topic-three-next">So what is technology, and what is AI?</h2>
      <div class="topic-landing__prose topic-landing__measure">
        <p>"A calling" is only the first answer. CTA also understands technology as an extension of ourselves and of our relationships, and AI as possibly something more: a new kind of creature. Each answer raises the stakes of how we respond.</p>
        <p><a href="/topics/what-is-technology-and-ai/">Read: What are technology and AI? &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="topic-landing__band" id="questions" aria-labelledby="topic-three-questions">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="topic-landing__section-label">Open questions</p>
      <h2 id="topic-three-questions">What this leaves open</h2>
      <div class="topic-landing__prose topic-landing__measure">
        <ul>
          <li>If technology is a calling, what should Christians refuse to build?</li>
          <li>Can one person keep up in some areas, resist in others, and create in others? Where is the line?</li>
          <li>Who decides what counts as turning a technology toward the work of God?</li>
          <li>What does Creative Engagement look like for someone who isn't an engineer?</li>
        </ul>
        <p>These are the questions we keep working on, and the ones readers bring us shape what we write next. If a question is still open for you, send it to us.</p>
        <p><a class="btn btn-default" href="mailto:admin@christiantranshumanism.org?subject=A%20question%20about%20technology">Send us your question</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
